// src/v4/std/machine.dag
//
// Scope: machine representation constraints — fixed-width words, bytes,
//        pointer width. The physical axis abstract numeric types project
//        onto (a 64-bit Int is the abstract Int composed with a machine
//        width).
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_(computer_architecture)
//
// Owns:
//   - Byte; Word8 / Word16 / Word32 / Word64 / Word128
//   - MachineWidth — the width axis numeric refinements compose against
//   - PointerWidth — the platform-dependent address width
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Conj, Cardinality
//   - std/logic.dag: Bool (a byte is a fixed-cardinality group of bits)
//   - std/nat.dag: Nat (a width is a count)
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-3 (scalar/numeric stack). Replaces
//   part of the deleted std/primitive.dag; see src/v4/STRUCTURE.md
//   §"scalar/numeric concept decomposition".
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.std.machine


import v4.std.logic { Bool }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CONJ + CARDINALITY (std/node.dag). The frozen header’s “Consumes …
// Cardinality” names the **Cardinality connective** (one of the six type
// connectives), not a refinement TYPE. A `Byte` / `Word*` is a **Conj**
// nominal record whose `List<…>` field is the substrate’s finite-sequence
// carrier (`List` = `FreeMonoid` — std/collection.dag). Do **not** write
// `Cardinality` as a type name here; std/cardinality.dag owns refinement
// *types* (`Multiplicity`, …) — out of this file’s deps.
//
// LIST FIELD LENGTH vs NOMINAL WIDTH — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡):
// The `bits` / `bytes` fields are honest **unrefined** `List<…>` carriers:
// nominal `Byte` / `Word*` names assert IEEE/ISO word sizes (8 bits;
// 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 bytes), but the substrate does not yet attach a
// structural length witness to those fields — the same class of gap
// dsl/std/bit.dag documents for v3 study (not lifted). This is acceptable
// ONLY as a **formal** deferral (operator-ratified collection.dag pattern:
// Map + FiniteSet — three bridge properties + named trigger). Bridge
// properties:
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS block; the types below intentionally use bare
//       `List<Bit>` / `List<Byte>` without a fixed-length refinement.
//   (b) bounds on use — no consumer may read `Byte` / `Word*` and treat
//       `bits` / `bytes` spine length as **proven** to match the nominal
//       width without an explicit validator or Grounding-side check;
//       std/integer.dag + std/float.dag (post-#3185) MUST NOT assert
//       width-correctness from these lists alone — they compose with
//       `MachineWidth<Word*>` and discharge exact width at Grounding /
//       emission, or wait on the dissolution trigger. Pipeline passes
//       that need structurally exact bit/byte counts wait on the same
//       trigger (or carry an explicit Witness / bounded carrier from the
//       follow-up substrate).
//   (c) dissolution trigger — **fixed-cardinality refinement substrate
//       lands** (the closed-system path: std/cardinality.dag `Multiplicity`
//       + bounded `List`/record refinements wired in the substrate grammar
//       — e.g. alias `where` / phantom-Nat bounds / field cardinality on
//       Conj — whichever ratified form first closes the “list length ≠
//       nominal width” hole). A follow-up PR then either refines
//       `Byte { bits: … }` / `Word* { bytes: … }` to carry the witness or
//       replaces these fields with a single-authority bounded-sequence
//       carrier and **deletes this 🟡 block** when the gap is structurally
//       closed. STUDY precedent for the honest gap statement: dsl/std/
//       bit.dag header (v4 does not lift v3/v2 artifacts wholesale).
//
// NAT (std/nat.dag). Widths are counts in the external consensus sense
// (8, 16, … bits); this file names **nominal word witnesses** (`Word8` …)
// for the `MachineWidth<…>` phantom slot. Literal `Nat` magnitude in the
// type DAG is Grounding’s job when lowering `MachineWidth<Word64>` → 64
// — not a second Nat authority duplicated here (INVARIANTS P2).
//
// PRACTICE 4 (modeling-discipline.md §4). This module authors **no**
// `type T = V1 | V2 | …` sum with N ≥ 2. Carriers here are: (1) a **type
// alias** `Bit = Bool` (the machine-traversable edge to std/logic.dag’s
// sole classical-bit authority — INVARIANTS worked-example chain); (2)
// **Conj** nominal records `Byte`, `Word8` … `Word128`; (3) **opaque**
// nominal placeholders `MachineWidth<bits>`, `PointerWidth`. For each
// arity-1 Conj / opaque: Practice-4 dissolution checkpoint **N/A** (not
// a multi-variant coproduct). The closed set of five `Word*` names is a
// deliberate **nominal** enumeration of the standard byte-multiple word
// sizes — not an internal `F<N>` family that would need a `Nat` index
// refinement type outside this file’s contract (Practice 5 / Practice 7
// do not force dissolving these into one parameter — they are distinct
// authorities for `MachineWidth<Word*>` arguments, same spirit as dsl/std/
// bit.dag’s separate `Word16` / `Word32` / …).
//
// MACHINE-READABLE INHABITANCE (operator ruling 2026-05-16). No machine.dag
// type inhabits an algebra in this PR — there is no prose-only Monoid/
// Ring claim; width facts are nominal structure + phantom carriers only.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Bit — one classical bit (width = 1). Alias-identity to `Bool` from
// std/logic.dag — the v4 classical truth value IS the bit carrier
// (epistemic stacking; dsl/std/bit.dag’s `Bit = Classical` line, refreshed
// for v4’s `Bool` name). Downstream reads machine width through bytes
// built from `Bit`, not a parallel bit algebra.
//
// Coproduct dissolution — N/A: not a new sum; `Bool`’s 🟢 ledger lives in
// std/logic.dag. This declaration is only the substrate-visible alias edge.
type Bit = Bool


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Byte — an octet (IEEE / ISO byte = 8 bits — INVARIANTS integer-chain
// anchor). Field is `List<Bit>`; exact length-8 is **not** structurally
// enforced — see Modeling notes “LIST FIELD LENGTH … TRACKED SCAFFOLD”.
//
// Coproduct dissolution — N/A: Conj nominal (single constructor shape),
// not an N ≥ 2 internal sum.
type Byte {
  bits: List<Bit>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Word8 / Word16 / Word32 / Word64 / Word128 — machine words as byte-
// sequences (Wikipedia “Word (computer architecture)” — anchor). Nominal
// widths 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 bytes; fields are `List<Byte>` without
// structural length enforcement — same 🟡 tracked scaffold as `Byte`
// (see Modeling notes).
//
// Coproduct dissolution — N/A for each: Conj nominal, not a sum.
type Word8 {
  bytes: List<Byte>
}

type Word16 {
  bytes: List<Byte>
}

type Word32 {
  bytes: List<Byte>
}

type Word64 {
  bytes: List<Byte>
}

type Word128 {
  bytes: List<Byte>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MachineWidth<bits> — sole machine-width **axis** carrier for composing
// abstract numeric types with a concrete width (e.g. future
// `Compose<Int, MachineWidth<Word64>>` in std/integer.dag). `bits` is a
// **phantom** type parameter — documentation intent: instantiated only
// with word nominals (`Word8` … `Word128`) or `PointerWidth`, not an
// arbitrary type expression. Shapes like `MachineWidth<String>` are not
// valid substrate uses; rejection is **mechanical at Grounding** (fail-
// closed / omit per universal-substrate posture — gunbc#828), not silent
// acceptance. Parser-parallel Nat bound on `bits` is a known P2/P5
// scaffold gap — dissolves in the same change set that first lands bounded
// phantom parameters or an explicit Nat-indexed width carrier; then delete
// this paragraph’s trigger text (mirror: dsl/std/machine_constraints.dag).
//
// Coproduct dissolution — N/A: zero-variant opaque nominal phantom; not a
// sum (Practice 4 applies to multi-variant coproducts only).
type MachineWidth<bits>


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PointerWidth — substrate token for the **target-resolved** pointer-width
// axis at concept layer (orthogonal to OS “platform” identity). Consumed
// as `MachineWidth<PointerWidth>`; Grounding projects to 32 / 64 / … per
// target. Same constrained-inhabitance / Grounding-enforcement posture as
// `MachineWidth<bits>` (gunbc#1739, #828 — rename rationale in dsl/std/
// machine_constraints.dag).
//
// Coproduct dissolution — N/A: zero-variant opaque nominal token; not a sum.
type PointerWidth
